  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cc80befa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Step 4. Split each credit into separate artists\n",
    "\n",
    "\"Bruno Mars & Cardi B\" is two artists. To count each artist's history, we lowercase the credit,\n",
    "replace every separator Billboard uses with `|`, and split on `|`. A few names contain a separator\n",
    "but are really one act, so we protect those by rewriting them first.\n",
    "\n",
    "We tested every protected name from an earlier, longer list. Only these three change any 2018+ song,\n",
    "so the list stays short."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "id": "2efb680e",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.628449Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.628386Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.630186Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.629842Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Names that contain a separator but are one act. We rewrite them so the split leaves them whole.\n",
    "protected_names = {\n",
    "    # \" x \" would cut \"Lil Nas X & Jack Harlow\" in the wrong place.\n",
    "    'lil nas x': 'lil nas-x',\n",
    "    # Keeps the 1960s duo out of the UK rapper Dave's history.\n",
    "    'sam & dave': 'sam and dave',\n",
    "    # Keeps the duo's hits out of Cher's solo history.\n",
    "    'sonny & cher': 'sonny and cher',\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Words Billboard puts between artists, including its own typos (\"Feauring\").\n",
    "# Longer phrases come first, so \" a duet with \" is removed before \" with \".\n",
    "separators = [' a duet with ', ' duet with ', ' featuring ', ' feauring ', ' featauring ',\n",
    "              ' feat. ', ' feat ', ' ft. ', ' with ', ' w/ ', ' vs. ', ' vs ',\n",
    "              ' introducing ', ' presents ', ' x ', ' & ', ', ', ': ', ' (']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "id": "1c7e4b12",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.630965Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.630910Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.718294Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-19T00:26:35.717961Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "most artists in one credit: 10\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Lowercase each credit and remove quote marks and closing brackets.\n",
    "names = songs['artist'].str.lower()\n",
    "names = names.str.replace('\"', '', regex=False).str.replace(')', '', regex=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Protect the one-act names first, then turn every separator into '|'.\n",
    "for name in protected_names:\n",
    "    names = names.str.replace(name, protected_names[name], regex=False)\n",
    "for separator in separators:\n",
    "    names = names.str.replace(separator, '|', regex=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Split on '|': one column per artist. The first column is the lead artist.\n",
    "artist_columns = names.str.split('|', expand=True)\n",
    "songs['primary_artist'] = artist_columns[0].str.strip()\n",
    "# How many artists the credit names (used in the recap, Q6b).\n",
    "songs['n_credited_artists'] = artist_columns.notna().sum(axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "print('most artists in one credit:', artist_columns.shape[1])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "558cf16a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We also mark songs with a featured artist. We look for each spelling of \"featuring\" with plain\n",
    "text search, no regex."
